Each algorithm (DirectSolver, ColumnGeneration) previously defined its own strategy interface that every problem had to implement. Adding a new problem required one class per algorithm; adding a new algorithm required all problems to grow a new implementation. This made the framework non-extensible.

## Core change: `MILPModel` + `IMILPFormulator`

**`src/utils/milp_model.hpp`** — new shared data layer:
- `VarDef` — name, bounds, type (`B`/`I`/`C`), objective coefficient
- `ConstrDef` — name, terms `(varName, coeff)`, sense, RHS
- `MILPModel` — variables + constraints + `objSense`
- `IMILPFormulator` — single interface every problem implements: `Formulate() → MILPModel` + `PrintSolution(...)`

## Problem side

`CuttingStockMILPFormulator` (replaces `CuttingStockDirectSolverStrategy`) implements `IMILPFormulator` — loads data and returns a self-contained `MILPModel`. The problem header no longer includes any algorithm header.

```cpp
// To add a new problem, implement one interface:
class MyProblemFormulator : public IMILPFormulator {
    MILPModel Formulate() override { /* build vars + constraints */ }
    void PrintSolution(const MILPModel&, const std::vector<double>&) override { ... }
};
```

## Algorithm side

`DirectSolver::Solve(const MILPModel&)` translates `MILPModel` to Gurobi variables/constraints generically — zero problem-specific logic. Registering a new problem is one line in the factory map:

```cpp
// direct_solver.cpp
static const std::map<ProblemType, std::function<std::unique_ptr<IMILPFormulator>()>> directFormulatorMap = {
    { CUTTINGSTOCK, []() { return std::make_unique<CuttingStockMILPFormulator>(); } },
    { MY_PROBLEM,   []() { return std::make_unique<MyProblemFormulator>(); } },  // ← one line
};
```

Future algorithms (Benders decomposition, etc.) consume the same `MILPModel` and apply their own decomposition without requiring problems to change.

